<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pliva</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pliva River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pliva is a river in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a> that flows through the Pliva lakes and joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vrbas">Vrbas</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jajce">Jajce</a> in a waterfall over 20 metres high in the centre of the town. Its valley formed part of the defences of Jajce, the royal Bosnian fortress that became the seat of a Hungarian banate after 1463, guarding the route into central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Pliva flows from west to east for 26.8 kilometres and drains a watershed of 1,326 square kilometres. It springs from two strong karstic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> at 483 metres above sea level, at the foot of the mountain of Smiljevac-Jastrebnjak near Pljeva, some 8 kilometres upstream of the town of Šipovo. In the centre of Šipovo it receives its main tributary, the Janj, which joins from the right carrying about as much water as the Pliva itself. As a left tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vrbas">Vrbas</a>, which flows north into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a>, it belongs to the Sava basin.</p><p>About 12 kilometres downstream, at the village of Jezero, the river enters Veliko Plivsko Lake and then the smaller Malo Plivsko and Okruglo lakes. From Jezero to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vrbas">Vrbas</a> its bed is of tufa, or travertine, laid down by moss, algae and bacteria in barriers that grow about 1 centimetre a year, as on the nearby Una River and at the Plitvice Lakes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>For hundreds of years the Pliva region was the last stronghold of the Bosnian Kingdom, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jajce">Jajce</a> as the permanent seat of its last kings. The first hydroelectric power station in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>, and the first in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, Jajce-1, began working on the Pliva on 24 March 1899. Built by the Austrian industrialist Dr. Josef Kranz and his company Bosnische-Elektrizitäts AG, it had a capacity of 8 MW and supplied a calcium carbide factory at Jajce, then the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, Jajce-2 was built on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vrbas">Vrbas</a> below the town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Between the two Plivsko lakes stands a cluster of small wooden watermills, used by local farmers to grind wheat until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and some afterwards, with their heyday in the days of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>; they are kept as a national monument of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>. The river's clean water and fish draw fly fishermen, and part of the 16th FIPS-Mouche European Fly Fishing Championship was held on the lake.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pliva (river)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The banate was essentially a chain of garrisoned castles along the Vrbas and <b>Pliva</b> valleys, financed by the Hungarian crown and commanded by bans such as Emeric Szapolyai.</blockquote><a href="/state/banate-of-jajce">Banate of Jajce</a></li></ul></section>
